            <ArticleTitle>Novel use of ultrasound in the ED: ultrasound-guided hematoma block of a proximal humeral fracture.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>130.e1-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ajem.2014.06.035</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0735-6757(14)00487-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Proximal humeral fractures are a common injury after falls, particularly in the elderly population. An ultrasound-guided hematoma block is a novel technique for analgesia in cases when standard intravenous analgesia is not efficacious. We present a case in which ultrasound-guided hematoma block was the ideal method for adjunctive pain control in a patient with a comminuted humeral head fracture.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
